[?2004l[?2004h> === TIMEZONE FIX SUMMARY ===
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> PROBLEM:
[?2004l[?2004h> --------
[?2004l[?2004h> The gen_report.py script was generating report filenames with incorrect timestamps.
[?2004l[?2004h> When run from cron (which may have TZ=UTC or no TZ set), the script would use
[?2004l[?2004h> UTC time instead of the configured Asia/Tokyo timezone, resulting in timestamps
[?2004l[?2004h> like 00:00:00 instead of 09:00:00 (9 hour difference).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ROOT CAUSE:
[?2004l[?2004h> -----------
[?2004l[?2004h> The script was reading /etc/timezone and setting os.environ['TZ'], but Python's
[?2004l[?2004h> datetime module doesn't automatically pick up TZ environment changes after the
[?2004l[?2004h> process starts. Simply setting os.environ['TZ'] is not enough.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> SOLUTION:
[?2004l[?2004h> ---------
[?2004l[?2004h> Added two critical changes to gen_report.py:
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. Import the 'time' module
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. Call time.tzset() immediately after setting os.environ['TZ']
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> This forces Python to reload timezone information and apply the new TZ setting.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> CHANGES MADE:
[?2004l[?2004h> -------------
[?2004l[?2004h> - Line 4: Added 'import time'
[?2004l[?2004h> - Lines 12-13: Added time.tzset() call with explanatory comment
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> VERIFICATION:
[?2004l[?2004h> -------------
[?2004l[?2004h> Tested with TZ=UTC environment (simulating cron):
[?2004l[?2004h> - Without fix: datetime.now() returns 17:44:41 (UTC)
[?2004l[?2004h> - With fix: datetime.now() returns 02:44:41 (JST)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The script now correctly generates reports with Asia/Tokyo timestamps regardless
[?2004l[?2004h> of the environment TZ setting when run from cron.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Backup of original script saved at: /home/user/scheduler/gen_report.py.backup
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
[?2004l[?2004hroot@bc14cb490303:/home/user# cat /home/user/scheduler/FIX_SUMMARY.txt
[?2004l=== TIMEZONE FIX SUMMARY ===

PROBLEM:
--------
The gen_report.py script was generating report filenames with incorrect timestamps.
When run from cron (which may have TZ=UTC or no TZ set), the script would use
UTC time instead of the configured Asia/Tokyo timezone, resulting in timestamps
like 00:00:00 instead of 09:00:00 (9 hour difference).

ROOT CAUSE:
-----------
The script was reading /etc/timezone and setting os.environ['TZ'], but Python's
datetime module doesn't automatically pick up TZ environment changes after the
process starts. Simply setting os.environ['TZ'] is not enough.

SOLUTION:
---------
Added two critical changes to gen_report.py:
1. Import the 'time' module
2. Call time.tzset() immediately after setting os.environ['TZ']

This forces Python to reload timezone information and apply the new TZ setting.

CHANGES MADE:
-------------
- Line 4: Added 'import time'
- Lines 12-13: Added time.tzset() call with explanatory comment

VERIFICATION:
-------------
Tested with TZ=UTC environment (simulating cron):
- Without fix: datetime.now() returns 17:44:41 (UTC)
- With fix: datetime.now() returns 02:44:41 (JST)

The script now correctly generates reports with Asia/Tokyo timestamps regardless
of the environment TZ setting when run from cron.

Backup of original script saved at: /home/user/scheduler/gen_report.py.backup
